{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And Pinchas saw.</b> He saw the deed and recalled the halakhah: he said to him [Mosheh], 'Did you not teach me thus when you descended from Sinai: he who lies with an Aramean woman — zealots may strike him?' He [Mosheh] said to him: 'Let the reader of the letter be the agent' — immediately, <b>and he arose from among the assembly and took a spear in his hand.</b> And in reward for this, the gifts were given to him, namely the arm, the cheek, and the stomach: the arm corresponding to 'and he took a spear in his hand,' the cheek corresponding to (Psalms 106:30) 'and Pinchas stood and prayed,' the stomach corresponding to 'and the woman — to her stomach.' And this commandment benefited Pinchas in this world and in the world to come: in this world, the gifts and the establishment of the priesthood for his seed, and further that he lived and prolonged his days in this world four hundred years, as it is written (Judges 11:26) 'while Israel dwelt in Heshbon and her daughters, and in Aroer and her daughters, three hundred years,' and we find from Yiftah to Gibeah of Benjamin one hundred years, and at that time it is written (Judges 20:28) 'and Pinchas son of Elazar stood before Him in those days.' In the world to come, in that the attribute of peace was given him, and when he departed after four hundred years he merited eternal life. And twelve miracles were performed in this matter: it was their nature to separate one from the other, and the angel joined them; He sealed their mouths so they would not cry out; He directed the iron opposite their nakedness; He lengthened the iron so that it would pierce them both; the angel raised the lintel so that the two of them would go out suspended before the eyes of all; He turned them in their natural position at the head of the iron; He gave strength to his arm to carry them, and strength to the staff to bear them, and that they did not slip from the staff, and that none of the blood spilled upon him, and that he hung them before the eyes of all. And Pinchas should have become impure with the impurity of conjunction, since he was touching the spear that touched the corpse. And it is known that if Reuven touched a corpse he is impure, and Shimon who touched Reuven is impure, as it is said 'the soul that touches' — by means of what the impure person touched, it becomes impure; and this contact, if it is by conjunction — that is, that Shimon touches Reuven while Reuven is still touching the corpse — he is impure with seven-day impurity; if not by conjunction, after Reuven has separated from the corpse, there is only evening impurity; and a person who touches utensils that touch a corpse has seven-day impurity, from what is written concerning Midian (Numbers 31:24) 'and you shall wash your garments on the seventh day.' And the reason Pinchas did not become impure, though he was touching a utensil touching a corpse, is that the utensil, namely the spear, was flat, and flat wooden utensils do not receive impurity; or for the reason stated in the Midrash, that the Holy One, blessed be He, guarded their spirits so that they would not die and render impure — and this is the last miracle, completing the twelve I mentioned. And even according to the view of the Midrash that there was iron there and it did not render impure — since they did not die while the spear was still in his hand. And after all these miracles were performed for him and he brought them out suspended before the eyes of all, the tribe of Shimon surrounded him and stood at the entrance; they sought to lay hands on him, and then the plague began among the people, and this is what is said: <b>and those who died in the plague were twenty-four thousand</b> — and all of them were from the tribe of Shimon. And so one may learn from the great deficit we find in their count in the section of Pinchas compared to the count they had at first in the wilderness of Sinai. And that so many of Israel fell in this place, whereas in the sin of the calf no more than about three thousand men fell — for there it was only idolatry, but here there was both harlotry and idolatry and the degradation of Israel's holiness in lying with the gentiles; and Pinchas came and was zealous with the zeal of the Holy One, blessed be He, and turned back His wrath from Israel, and surrendered himself to danger against the whole family, who were a numerous host, when he saw the desecration of the Name in public. He paid no heed to the dignity of the wicked man, who was a prince of the tribe of Shimon, nor to the dignity of the Midianites, for she was a king's daughter, and was zealous for the honor of the Name, may He be exalted, in order to teach all creatures that the honor of all the world and its fullness belongs only to His honor, as it is written (Isaiah 43:7) 'every one that is called by My name, and for My honor I have created him, I have formed him, indeed I have made him.' And therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, is called the King of Glory, as David, peace be upon him, said of Him (Psalms 24:10) 'Who is He, this King of Glory? YHWH of hosts — He is the King of Glory. Selah.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The prooftext from Judges 11:26 is quoted partially; the citation writes ערער ובבנותיה ובחשבון ובבנותיה in reversed order from the verse which reads חשבון ובנותיה... ערער ובנותיה; rendered as he cites it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:36:14.553002188Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}